WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.665 --> 00:00:06.305
- I would never get too
hung up on mistakes.

00:00:06.305 --> 00:00:07.907
- I make mistakes all the time.

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:08.969
- I need mistakes.

00:00:08.969 --> 00:00:10.450
- Learn from those mistakes.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:11.826
- I have to learn from my mistakes.

00:00:11.826 --> 00:00:14.212
- I continually learn from my mistakes.

00:00:14.212 --> 00:00:16.446
- Learn from your mistakes.

00:00:16.446 --> 00:00:17.398
- Learn from your mistakes.

00:00:17.398 --> 00:00:19.082
- Hello Believe Nation.

00:00:19.082 --> 00:00:21.298
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:21.298 --> 00:00:22.606
My one word is believe and I believe

00:00:22.606 --> 00:00:23.886
that entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:23.886 --> 00:00:26.185
all the major problems of the world.

00:00:26.185 --> 00:00:27.931
So to help you on your journey today,

00:00:27.931 --> 00:00:29.837
we're going to cover how you can learn

00:00:29.837 --> 00:00:31.294
from your mistakes.

00:00:31.294 --> 00:00:32.764
And as always guys, if you hear something

00:00:32.764 --> 00:00:34.230
that really resonates with you,

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.138
please leave it down
in the Comments below.

00:00:36.138 --> 00:00:37.378
Put quotes around it so other people

00:00:37.378 --> 00:00:38.827
can be inspired as well.

00:00:38.827 --> 00:00:40.348
And when you write it down,

00:00:40.348 --> 00:00:44.515
it's much more likely to
stick for yourself too, enjoy.

00:00:45.801 --> 00:00:48.718
(orchestral music)

00:00:55.833 --> 00:00:57.046
- I was just wondering,

00:00:57.046 --> 00:00:58.263
what would you consider to be

00:00:58.263 --> 00:01:01.565
the worst investment you've ever made?

00:01:01.565 --> 00:01:03.237
- The worst investment I've ever made?

00:01:03.237 --> 00:01:04.859
How long do you have?

00:01:04.859 --> 00:01:06.891
(audience laughs)

00:01:06.891 --> 00:01:08.419
Why, I've made some very bad ones

00:01:08.419 --> 00:01:10.803
but that doesn't really bother me.

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:12.693
It may bother the shareholders,

00:01:12.693 --> 00:01:15.710
but that's another question.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.532
You're going to make mistakes in life.

00:01:17.532 --> 00:01:19.296
There's no question about it.

00:01:19.296 --> 00:01:20.982
You don't want to make them on
the big decisions, you know?

00:01:20.982 --> 00:01:22.836
Who you marry and things like that.

00:01:22.836 --> 00:01:24.192
So there's no way I'm going to make

00:01:24.192 --> 00:01:26.471
a lot of business and investment decisions

00:01:26.471 --> 00:01:27.677
without making some mistakes.

00:01:27.677 --> 00:01:29.683
I may try to minimize them.

00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:31.510
I don't dwell on them at all.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:32.698
I don't look back.

00:01:32.698 --> 00:01:36.225
The biggest mistakes are the ones

00:01:36.225 --> 00:01:38.021
that actually don't show up.

00:01:38.021 --> 00:01:40.220
They're mistakes of omission
rather than commission.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.198
We've never lost that much money

00:01:43.198 --> 00:01:45.689
on any one investment.

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:47.355
But it's the things
that I knew enough to do

00:01:47.355 --> 00:01:48.854
that I didn't do.

00:01:48.854 --> 00:01:52.210
We have missed profits
of as much as you know,

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.948
maybe 10 billion dollars and things.

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:56.587
That I knew enough to do, and I didn't do.

00:01:56.587 --> 00:01:59.823
Now, the fact that I didn't
buy Microsoft way back,

00:01:59.823 --> 00:02:01.938
is not a foregone opportunity

00:02:01.938 --> 00:02:04.282
cause I didn't know enough
to make that decision.

00:02:04.282 --> 00:02:06.934
But there have been other investments

00:02:06.934 --> 00:02:09.606
where I did know enough
to make the decision

00:02:09.606 --> 00:02:10.984
and for one reason or another,

00:02:10.984 --> 00:02:12.566
I either didn't do it at all

00:02:12.566 --> 00:02:14.098
or I did it on a small scale.

00:02:14.098 --> 00:02:15.409
I was sucking my thumb

00:02:15.409 --> 00:02:16.857
when I should have been writing checks,

00:02:16.857 --> 00:02:18.818
you know basically.

00:02:18.818 --> 00:02:20.451
And those don't show up.

00:02:20.451 --> 00:02:22.824
There's no place where it
shows missed opportunities

00:02:22.824 --> 00:02:24.896
but I've missed some big ones.

00:02:24.896 --> 00:02:28.522
The triumphs in life are partly triumphs

00:02:28.522 --> 00:02:29.418
because you know that everything

00:02:29.418 --> 00:02:31.191
isn't going to be a triumph.

00:02:31.191 --> 00:02:35.135
And I would never get
too hung up on mistakes.

00:02:35.135 --> 00:02:37.654
I know a lot of people that
really agonize over 'em.

00:02:37.654 --> 00:02:38.986
And it just isn't worth it.

00:02:38.986 --> 00:02:39.958
I mean, tomorrow's another day

00:02:39.958 --> 00:02:40.993
and you live it forward

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:42.747
and just go on to the next thing.

00:02:42.747 --> 00:02:44.125
- So many people come up to me

00:02:44.125 --> 00:02:45.574
and say, Gary it's so hard.

00:02:45.574 --> 00:02:46.690
I look at their Instagram

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:47.923
and they're not posting
any of their content

00:02:47.923 --> 00:02:50.613
with hashtags, which is a by accident way

00:02:50.613 --> 00:02:52.403
to pick up exposure in a world

00:02:52.403 --> 00:02:54.084
where they have no money.

00:02:54.084 --> 00:02:55.345
When you have no money, when I took over

00:02:55.345 --> 00:02:56.799
my dad's business, I did
three million dollars a year,

00:02:56.799 --> 00:03:00.044
10% gross profit,
$300,000 before expenses.

00:03:00.044 --> 00:03:02.780
My first year Marketing
budget was $14,000.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.029
When you have no money,

00:03:04.029 --> 00:03:05.515
and I built that business from a three

00:03:05.515 --> 00:03:07.873
to $60 million business in five years.

00:03:07.873 --> 00:03:09.951
I had to make every penny perfect.

00:03:09.951 --> 00:03:12.588
So I was right about
e-mail marketing and '96.

00:03:12.588 --> 00:03:13.973
And I was right and because of that,

00:03:13.973 --> 00:03:15.735
I had 91% open rates.

00:03:15.735 --> 00:03:17.266
When Google Ad Words came out,

00:03:17.266 --> 00:03:20.424
the day it came out, I paid
five cents a click for words

00:03:20.424 --> 00:03:23.005
before anybody bid me
up and I was super right

00:03:23.005 --> 00:03:24.118
and that worked.

00:03:24.118 --> 00:03:26.219
- How much is that luck verses preparation

00:03:26.219 --> 00:03:27.821
and do you ever get anything wrong?

00:03:27.821 --> 00:03:29.141
What do you do when you make a mistake?

00:03:29.141 --> 00:03:30.904
- I get everything wrong, it's just that

00:03:30.904 --> 00:03:32.828
I can't recall it, cause once it's wrong,

00:03:32.828 --> 00:03:34.630
I'm moving on to the next thing.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:37.682
Like, dwelling on what
you've fucked up on,

00:03:37.682 --> 00:03:42.402
is the quickest way for the
next thing not to work, right?

00:03:42.402 --> 00:03:44.845
So I think I do everything,

00:03:44.845 --> 00:03:47.236
I mean you know this, this
is a fun thing to say.

00:03:47.236 --> 00:03:48.542
Some people in the back know this.

00:03:48.542 --> 00:03:53.024
I was a break-out YouTube
star in the first year, 2006.

00:03:53.024 --> 00:03:54.802
I decided that the right strategy

00:03:54.802 --> 00:03:56.772
was to leave YouTube completely

00:03:56.772 --> 00:03:58.926
and go to Vidler because Vidler

00:03:58.926 --> 00:04:02.424
offered me equity in their company.

00:04:02.424 --> 00:04:04.106
And I've left an enormous
amount of attention,

00:04:04.106 --> 00:04:06.313
I deviated from my intention thesis

00:04:06.313 --> 00:04:10.056
to do short term economics
and equity in a company.

00:04:10.056 --> 00:04:11.262
And I lost, I lost.

00:04:11.262 --> 00:04:12.981
When DRock finally came
into my life two years ago

00:04:12.981 --> 00:04:13.884
and we started to try to

00:04:13.884 --> 00:04:15.153
build up my YouTube for the first time,

00:04:15.153 --> 00:04:17.277
I was sitting on 40,000 followers

00:04:17.277 --> 00:04:18.694
in a world where I could have had millions

00:04:18.694 --> 00:04:20.026
if I just stayed the course.

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:22.042
So I make mistakes all the time.

00:04:22.042 --> 00:04:23.902
I'm re-orging VaynerMedia every day

00:04:23.902 --> 00:04:26.463
cause it's based on a mistake
I made the prior year.

00:04:26.463 --> 00:04:28.688
I just don't give a
fuck about my mistakes.

00:04:28.688 --> 00:04:30.217
Everybody else cares about your mistakes.

00:04:30.217 --> 00:04:31.913
If you're worried about your own mistakes,

00:04:31.913 --> 00:04:32.971
you've already lost.

00:04:32.971 --> 00:04:35.023
- You know, I never
want to be always right

00:04:35.023 --> 00:04:38.590
cause then I can't grow, I can't learn.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:41.730
So, I have no desire to be always right,

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:43.528
I need mistakes you know?

00:04:43.528 --> 00:04:45.797
And luckily enough, and knock on

00:04:45.797 --> 00:04:47.651
this moving piano here that

00:04:47.651 --> 00:04:50.651
my mistakes are never crucial enough

00:04:51.754 --> 00:04:53.416
for me to be like, man, you know?

00:04:53.416 --> 00:04:56.171
They're always great learning experiences.

00:04:56.171 --> 00:04:59.987
- Before you succeed, you
must first learn to fail.

00:04:59.987 --> 00:05:02.110
You know, a lot of people have failed

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.888
and get down on themselves

00:05:04.888 --> 00:05:09.055
but the best of sports stars
learn from their mistakes.

00:05:10.008 --> 00:05:11.206
You know, I should have did this,

00:05:11.206 --> 00:05:12.601
okay we'll do that next year.

00:05:12.601 --> 00:05:14.077
And just keep learning until you get

00:05:14.077 --> 00:05:16.260
to the point to where you're the champion.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.313
Then everybody has to compete you.

00:05:18.313 --> 00:05:20.679
Took me eight years before
I got my first championship.

00:05:20.679 --> 00:05:22.629
Then after that, I won three in a row,

00:05:22.629 --> 00:05:25.325
lost two, then I got another one

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:27.766
and then kept losing, so it's not easy.

00:05:27.766 --> 00:05:32.111
But you know, it's not
a specific time period

00:05:32.111 --> 00:05:33.696
of how long it takes you, just have to

00:05:33.696 --> 00:05:35.447
learn from your mistakes.

00:05:35.447 --> 00:05:37.024
You make mistakes and then

00:05:37.024 --> 00:05:38.887
just try to capitalize on that.

00:05:38.887 --> 00:05:40.670
- [Narrator] Earlier this summer,

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:42.475
you were in a donut shop,
you were in California.

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:45.588
You said some hurtful
things about Americans

00:05:45.588 --> 00:05:47.369
and about people's weight.

00:05:47.369 --> 00:05:50.987
A lot of people watching
felt offended by your words.

00:05:50.987 --> 00:05:53.101
What do you want to say?

00:05:53.101 --> 00:05:56.089
- First of all, I mean my behavior was

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:58.946
very offensive and I apologize to those.

00:05:58.946 --> 00:06:01.001
No excuse or there's
nothing to justify it.

00:06:01.001 --> 00:06:05.068
But, you know I think
that as human beings,

00:06:05.068 --> 00:06:06.332
we all say and do things that

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:09.527
we don't mean at all, sometimes.

00:06:09.527 --> 00:06:10.868
And we have to learn from it,

00:06:10.868 --> 00:06:11.903
I mean that's part of our process.

00:06:11.903 --> 00:06:13.658
We have to learn from our mistakes

00:06:13.658 --> 00:06:15.179
and that's how we grow.

00:06:15.179 --> 00:06:17.680
- I continually learn from my mistakes

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.713
and other people's mistakes.

00:06:19.713 --> 00:06:21.801
See, entrepreneurs think that,

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:23.295
hey to create that blend, our company,

00:06:23.295 --> 00:06:24.708
our hundred million dollar company,

00:06:24.708 --> 00:06:25.731
or even million dollar company,

00:06:25.731 --> 00:06:27.648
you have to be very smart,

00:06:27.648 --> 00:06:30.151
and sure, brains is part of it,

00:06:30.151 --> 00:06:31.815
luck is a huge component.

00:06:31.815 --> 00:06:34.299
But what I've learned over the years is,

00:06:34.299 --> 00:06:36.180
everyone, no matter how smart you are,

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.663
you're going to fail many times
and you're going to make mistakes.

00:06:38.663 --> 00:06:40.165
Just look at Mark Zuckerberg.

00:06:40.165 --> 00:06:41.713
Facebook worked out.

00:06:41.713 --> 00:06:43.081
His version of Snapchat,

00:06:43.081 --> 00:06:46.509
that was a copycat, did
not work out, right?

00:06:46.509 --> 00:06:49.236
So there's other areas
in which Facebook failed.

00:06:49.236 --> 00:06:50.528
But Facebook overall as a company

00:06:50.528 --> 00:06:51.971
has done great.

00:06:51.971 --> 00:06:53.436
Just because you're smart doesn't mean

00:06:53.436 --> 00:06:55.182
you're always going to succeed

00:06:55.182 --> 00:06:56.515
with everything you try or touch.

00:06:56.515 --> 00:06:58.753
But, every time you do something,

00:06:58.753 --> 00:07:00.582
you will learn new things,

00:07:00.582 --> 00:07:01.796
you'll make mistakes

00:07:01.796 --> 00:07:03.254
and if you learn from these mistakes

00:07:03.254 --> 00:07:06.457
and avoid making them over and over again,

00:07:06.457 --> 00:07:08.120
you'll actually increase your odds

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.209
of succeeding as time goes on.

00:07:10.209 --> 00:07:11.358
And the best way to do this

00:07:11.358 --> 00:07:13.322
is not just to learn from your mistakes

00:07:13.322 --> 00:07:16.191
but it's also to learn from
the mistakes of others.

00:07:16.191 --> 00:07:18.414
Rockefeller, Carnegie, all these people

00:07:18.414 --> 00:07:20.719
who are entrepreneurs,
years ago in the 1800's,

00:07:20.719 --> 00:07:22.636
1900's, they themselves

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:25.847
used business principals and concepts

00:07:25.847 --> 00:07:28.538
that are still applicable
in today's world.

00:07:28.538 --> 00:07:29.668
Sure, we have the Internet.

00:07:29.668 --> 00:07:31.710
But the same strategies
and concepts that they used

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:33.747
are still applicable, right?

00:07:33.747 --> 00:07:34.924
So learn from their mistakes,

00:07:34.924 --> 00:07:36.129
learn what worked for them,

00:07:36.129 --> 00:07:37.976
what didn't work for them, why?

00:07:37.976 --> 00:07:40.730
And bring that into
what you're doing today.

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:43.215
- You haven't gotten
every investment right?

00:07:43.215 --> 00:07:46.199
Tell me about the worst
misses that you've had

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:48.218
or sort of, the biggest setbacks there.

00:07:48.218 --> 00:07:49.567
- Every investment you get into,

00:07:49.567 --> 00:07:51.482
you're getting in there
because you think you have

00:07:51.482 --> 00:07:53.787
an airtight thesis for why this
thing is going to work out,

00:07:53.787 --> 00:07:55.056
you're convicted, you're passionate,

00:07:55.056 --> 00:07:56.568
you're putting not just your money in,

00:07:56.568 --> 00:07:58.901
but all your time and effort

00:07:59.807 --> 00:08:02.809
and your own brand and your
reputation is on the line.

00:08:02.809 --> 00:08:05.232
And more often than not, we're wrong.

00:08:05.232 --> 00:08:08.487
But even more painful are
some of these ones where

00:08:08.487 --> 00:08:11.171
I didn't invest at all,
and they've gone on

00:08:11.171 --> 00:08:12.809
to be the mega unicorns.

00:08:12.809 --> 00:08:15.229
I think about Airbnb.

00:08:15.229 --> 00:08:16.523
When they told me the pitch

00:08:16.523 --> 00:08:18.501
about renting out a room
in somebody else's house

00:08:18.501 --> 00:08:20.456
while the owner was still there,

00:08:20.456 --> 00:08:21.824
I literally looked those guys in the eye

00:08:21.824 --> 00:08:24.380
and I said, you guys,
this is so dangerous, man.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:25.927
Somebody's going to get raped or murdered

00:08:25.927 --> 00:08:27.570
and the blood is going
to be on your hands,

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.471
I think this is just a really bad idea.

00:08:30.471 --> 00:08:31.737
I think they're currently raising

00:08:31.737 --> 00:08:33.332
at a 20 billion dollar evaluation now,

00:08:33.332 --> 00:08:35.597
so my fund probably missed out on

00:08:35.597 --> 00:08:37.723
hundreds of millions of dollars
as a result of that, right?

00:08:37.723 --> 00:08:41.797
- You have to learn from your mistakes.

00:08:41.797 --> 00:08:44.616
I have this attitude that
no situation is impossible.

00:08:44.616 --> 00:08:46.298
There is nothing impossible.

00:08:46.298 --> 00:08:49.194
They're just continued seas
and obstacles to get around.

00:08:49.194 --> 00:08:50.944
So if I went out and,

00:08:53.324 --> 00:08:54.668
I'm just tryin' to think of obstacles.

00:08:54.668 --> 00:08:57.720
Let's just say you did an opener
and she'd hear it already.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:58.997
You did your lines, she'd heard it already

00:08:58.997 --> 00:09:00.398
because your friend just approached her

00:09:00.398 --> 00:09:01.400
with it 10 minutes ago.

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:02.452
Do you run screaming?

00:09:02.452 --> 00:09:03.550
Maybe that happens the first time.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:04.801
The second time, you say,
if that happens again,

00:09:04.801 --> 00:09:05.842
what do I do?

00:09:05.842 --> 00:09:07.155
You say, oh my God, you
must have met my friend,

00:09:07.155 --> 00:09:09.444
that's really funny, we were
just talking about that.

00:09:09.444 --> 00:09:11.079
Bam, problem solved.

00:09:11.079 --> 00:09:11.999
Whatever it may be.

00:09:11.999 --> 00:09:14.851
Say you, any problem has a solution.

00:09:14.851 --> 00:09:16.344
So I kind of have that attitude.

00:09:16.344 --> 00:09:18.621
You know, what if she's with eight guys?

00:09:18.621 --> 00:09:22.985
What if you know, she is
on her way out the door?

00:09:22.985 --> 00:09:24.422
Any problem is going to have a solution.

00:09:24.422 --> 00:09:26.683
So you have to have that attitude.

00:09:26.683 --> 00:09:28.798
And, if something goes wrong,

00:09:28.798 --> 00:09:29.923
and I'm saying this in a positive way,

00:09:29.923 --> 00:09:31.083
not a negative self-esteem way,

00:09:31.083 --> 00:09:32.523
but if something goes wrong,

00:09:32.523 --> 00:09:34.340
it's always your fault,
that's what I feel like.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:35.501
Without being hard on myself,

00:09:35.501 --> 00:09:37.156
if something goes wrong,
it's never her fault.

00:09:37.156 --> 00:09:38.488
She's not a bitch,

00:09:38.488 --> 00:09:41.762
she is not mean, she is not uptight,

00:09:41.762 --> 00:09:44.181
I did something wrong to
make her uncomfortable.

00:09:44.181 --> 00:09:47.135
So for example, with the openers,

00:09:47.135 --> 00:09:48.609
I would start out, walking up and saying

00:09:48.609 --> 00:09:50.302
hey guys, I want to get
an opinion on something,

00:09:50.302 --> 00:09:51.704
I've got this question I'm trying to ask

00:09:51.704 --> 00:09:52.876
and I'd start asking it.

00:09:52.876 --> 00:09:54.738
And I noticed they
weren't really listening,

00:09:54.738 --> 00:09:56.164
they were tuning me out,

00:09:56.164 --> 00:09:58.432
they were kind of giving
me bad body language.

00:09:58.432 --> 00:09:59.844
So I realized, what are they thinking?

00:09:59.844 --> 00:10:00.737
Why is that happening?

00:10:00.737 --> 00:10:02.267
Well it's happening because
I'm standing around,

00:10:02.267 --> 00:10:03.686
they don't know when I'm going to leave.

00:10:03.686 --> 00:10:05.281
So I added what's called
a time constraint.

00:10:05.281 --> 00:10:06.289
I'd walk in, I'd say,

00:10:06.289 --> 00:10:08.193
I want to get a quick
opinion on something,

00:10:08.193 --> 00:10:09.330
by the way I can only stay for a second,

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.823
cause I got to re-join
my friends over there,

00:10:11.823 --> 00:10:13.271
you know, problem solved.

00:10:13.271 --> 00:10:15.852
So, anytime somethin' went wrong,

00:10:15.852 --> 00:10:17.725
or maybe I would do everything,

00:10:17.725 --> 00:10:19.937
and I felt like I was doing it perfectly,

00:10:19.937 --> 00:10:21.135
but people weren't interested,

00:10:21.135 --> 00:10:23.124
while I was doing it,
doing all the right things

00:10:23.124 --> 00:10:25.318
but I was doing it in a very needy way.

00:10:25.318 --> 00:10:26.999
A lot of people will read the material

00:10:26.999 --> 00:10:28.657
and they'll say, this doesn't work.

00:10:28.657 --> 00:10:30.232
Everything works.

00:10:30.232 --> 00:10:32.642
What I say is going to be
different than what Zan says

00:10:32.642 --> 00:10:33.804
and different from what someone else says.

00:10:33.804 --> 00:10:36.374
But it all works, you
just have to do it right.

00:10:36.374 --> 00:10:39.263
So for example, the opinion
openers we're talking about,

00:10:39.263 --> 00:10:41.623
an opinion opener is how
we start a conversation.

00:10:41.623 --> 00:10:42.847
If you go and you say,

00:10:42.847 --> 00:10:45.372
can I ask you a question on something

00:10:45.372 --> 00:10:48.470
and then they say, no
we're talking, get lost.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:50.761
Well, the opinion openers don't work,

00:10:50.761 --> 00:10:51.826
you just did it wrong.

00:10:51.826 --> 00:10:53.075
You walk and you say,

00:10:53.075 --> 00:10:54.778
hey guys I want to ask a
quick question on something.

00:10:54.778 --> 00:10:56.253
Now you haven't asked
them a yes no question

00:10:56.253 --> 00:10:57.573
and it's going to work.

00:10:57.573 --> 00:10:58.962
So there's so many little subtleties,

00:10:58.962 --> 00:11:00.419
does that make sense?

00:11:00.419 --> 00:11:01.729
Okay so there's somebody else,

00:11:01.729 --> 00:11:02.956
I saw somebody looking at me strangely,

00:11:02.956 --> 00:11:04.122
so there's so many little subtleties

00:11:04.122 --> 00:11:04.981
that you have to work in

00:11:04.981 --> 00:11:08.604
and it's always, always
got to be your fault

00:11:08.604 --> 00:11:09.443
if it's not working.

00:11:09.443 --> 00:11:10.276
And not in a negative way,

00:11:10.276 --> 00:11:12.080
not in being hard on yourself.

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:14.651
It's feedback so you can improve yourself.

00:11:14.651 --> 00:11:16.891
That whole idea, there's
no such thing as failure,

00:11:16.891 --> 00:11:18.322
but only learning lessons.

00:11:18.322 --> 00:11:19.189
That's what I did but you know what?

00:11:19.189 --> 00:11:20.160
I'm a guy who's hard on myself.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.156
But I'm hard on myself cause
it makes me excel usually.

00:11:22.156 --> 00:11:24.425
- It's been a long time

00:11:24.425 --> 00:11:27.342
since I told stories about my boat.

00:11:28.849 --> 00:11:30.819
But Jay and I bought a schooner,

00:11:30.819 --> 00:11:32.773
the schooner Elizabeth.

00:11:32.773 --> 00:11:34.748
And this is the bell off from it

00:11:34.748 --> 00:11:38.197
and it's only important
because the boat sank.

00:11:38.197 --> 00:11:42.047
She was kind of a nice little
boat but she leaked a lot.

00:11:42.047 --> 00:11:43.857
That was our first big
sailing experiences.

00:11:43.857 --> 00:11:46.602
But we bought it, we went on it,

00:11:46.602 --> 00:11:49.719
and we sank and owned
many boats since then.

00:11:49.719 --> 00:11:51.584
You learn from mistakes.

00:11:51.584 --> 00:11:54.504
You got to start with faith
and belief in yourself

00:11:54.504 --> 00:11:57.943
but that's somethin' you got
to make it your business to do.

00:11:57.943 --> 00:11:59.642
- Knowledge is priceless okay?

00:11:59.642 --> 00:12:00.814
Even when you make mistakes

00:12:00.814 --> 00:12:02.022
and you learn from your mistakes.

00:12:02.022 --> 00:12:04.225
I lost half a million
dollars on one stock,

00:12:04.225 --> 00:12:06.545
I invested in my best
friend's dad's company,

00:12:06.545 --> 00:12:08.923
which basically invented
print at home ticketing.

00:12:08.923 --> 00:12:11.871
They went bankrupt and CEO,

00:12:11.871 --> 00:12:12.927
evil person.

00:12:12.927 --> 00:12:14.048
(laughs)

00:12:14.048 --> 00:12:16.103
CEO bought them in bankruptcy,

00:12:16.103 --> 00:12:17.351
changed the company name,

00:12:17.351 --> 00:12:18.538
kept all the same contracts,

00:12:18.538 --> 00:12:20.762
they re-sold the company
for like, 30 million.

00:12:20.762 --> 00:12:22.123
I would have made like, 10 million

00:12:22.123 --> 00:12:24.090
but I got wiped out.

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:25.394
It was a good lesson for me,

00:12:25.394 --> 00:12:26.836
I broke my trading rules

00:12:26.836 --> 00:12:29.006
and I didn't really know, you know,

00:12:29.006 --> 00:12:30.526
at the time, I was
like, batting a thousand

00:12:30.526 --> 00:12:31.433
and I had that loss.

00:12:31.433 --> 00:12:33.960
So your losses, your
mistakes make you better.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:35.831
I tell this story about Pete Sampras,

00:12:35.831 --> 00:12:36.735
you know Pete Sampras?

00:12:36.735 --> 00:12:37.972
- Yes.

00:12:37.972 --> 00:12:40.442
- All right, when he was
a junior in I don't know,

00:12:40.442 --> 00:12:42.886
I want to say like, '13, '14', '15,

00:12:42.886 --> 00:12:44.921
he was one of the top
ranked players in the U.S.

00:12:44.921 --> 00:12:47.286
But his coach had a
radical transformation plan

00:12:47.286 --> 00:12:48.459
to get him to number one.

00:12:48.459 --> 00:12:50.398
And he said Pete, hit the
ball as hard as you can.

00:12:50.398 --> 00:12:51.752
Do you remember this story?

00:12:51.752 --> 00:12:52.585
- No I don't.

00:12:52.585 --> 00:12:53.669
- He hit the ball as hard as he can

00:12:53.669 --> 00:12:55.297
and Pete's like, but
I'm not going to be able

00:12:55.297 --> 00:12:56.296
to control it if I do that.

00:12:56.296 --> 00:12:58.861
I was a tennis player
so I hear this story.

00:12:58.861 --> 00:12:59.730
And the coach is like, do you trust me?

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:01.028
And Pete's like, yeah I do.

00:13:01.028 --> 00:13:02.097
So hit the ball as hard as you can.

00:13:02.097 --> 00:13:03.971
So for like, I want to
say six, nine months,

00:13:03.971 --> 00:13:05.489
maybe even a year, Pete hit the ball

00:13:05.489 --> 00:13:06.427
as hard as he could

00:13:06.427 --> 00:13:07.595
and the balls were flying out,

00:13:07.595 --> 00:13:09.240
his ranking dropped, he
was like, number six,

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:10.764
he dropped to like, 200.

00:13:10.764 --> 00:13:12.480
He was just like, nobody right?

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.660
But then he gradually learned control

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:18.097
and he became number one
far and above anybody else.

00:13:18.097 --> 00:13:20.354
So, sometimes you have to lose

00:13:20.354 --> 00:13:22.925
or lose your stature at first.

00:13:22.925 --> 00:13:24.603
And then come back stronger.

00:13:24.603 --> 00:13:26.222
And come back so much better.

00:13:26.222 --> 00:13:27.442
But it's tough for people to accept

00:13:27.442 --> 00:13:28.524
because people just want success.

00:13:28.524 --> 00:13:30.522
They think it's like, gradually building

00:13:30.522 --> 00:13:31.864
but they don't, you know success,

00:13:31.864 --> 00:13:33.465
there's an info-graphic that I post

00:13:33.465 --> 00:13:35.805
on Instagram and it's
like, this is success.

00:13:35.805 --> 00:13:36.840
- Yeah exactly.

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:38.135
- But eventually you learn everything.

00:13:38.135 --> 00:13:39.935
So if you make 50 mistakes,

00:13:39.935 --> 00:13:41.518
you learn 50 things not to do,

00:13:41.518 --> 00:13:42.886
that makes you wiser.

00:13:42.886 --> 00:13:44.560
But most people learn what not to do

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:45.936
and then they give up.

00:13:45.936 --> 00:13:47.543
Like, heh this is so frustrating.

00:13:47.543 --> 00:13:49.109
So you can't ever give up.

00:13:49.109 --> 00:13:50.381
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:50.381 --> 00:13:51.429
I hope you enjoyed.

00:13:51.429 --> 00:13:53.072
I'd love to know, what was your biggest

00:13:53.072 --> 00:13:54.869
takeaway from this video?

00:13:54.869 --> 00:13:56.779
What are you going to immediately apply

00:13:56.779 --> 00:13:59.073
somehow to your life or to your business?

00:13:59.073 --> 00:14:00.359
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:14:00.359 --> 00:14:01.681
I'm super curious to find out.

00:14:01.681 --> 00:14:02.904
I also want to give a quick shout out

00:14:02.904 --> 00:14:05.342
to Craig Severinsen from Bright Works PR.

00:14:05.342 --> 00:14:06.955
Craig, thank you so much
for picking up a copy

00:14:06.955 --> 00:14:08.709
of my book, Your One Word.

00:14:08.709 --> 00:14:10.454
I really, really, really appreciate it

00:14:10.454 --> 00:14:12.144
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:14:12.144 --> 00:14:13.638
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:14:13.638 --> 00:14:14.860
I believe in you,

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:16.673
I hope you get from me
to believe in yourself

00:14:16.673 --> 00:14:18.814
and whatever your one word is,

00:14:18.814 --> 00:14:21.231
much love, I'll see you soon.

